WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.880
Power Query has hundreds of functions, but sometimes you need your own.

2
00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:09.640
In this lesson, you will learn to write a simple function in a blank

3
00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:15.080
query, fix its syntax errors, and use it to create a new column for every row.

4
00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:16.440
Let's build a tool!

5
00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:21.911
On the Home tab, open New Source and choose Blank Query.

6
00:00:21.911 --> 00:00:25.194
We get an empty query with just a formula bar.

7
00:00:25.194 --> 00:00:27.820
This is where we will write our function.

8
00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:03.151
First, I check the Financial Sample.

9
00:01:03.151 --> 00:01:07.460
I want a function that multiplies Units Sold by Sale Price.

10
00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:43.541
In the blank query, I type the inputs in brackets:

11
00:01:43.541 --> 00:01:49.525
Units Sold and Sale Price, then the arrow, an equals sign and a greater than sign.

12
00:01:49.525 --> 00:01:51.770
After the arrow comes the calculation:

13
00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:53.640
Units Sold times Sale Price.

14
00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:20.591
An error appears: Token comma expected.

15
00:02:20.591 --> 00:02:25.243
Spaces in names confuse M, so I use an underscore:

16
00:02:25.243 --> 00:02:28.500
Units underscore Sold, and Sale underscore Price.

17
00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:48.179
Now the function is valid, and Power Query shows a small form to test it.

18
00:02:48.179 --> 00:02:52.840
In the Financial Sample, I rename the columns to match the function inputs.

19
00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:11.493
On the Add Column tab, I choose Invoke Custom Function.

20
00:03:11.493 --> 00:03:18.140
I name the new column, pick the function, and choose which column goes into each input. Click OK.

21
00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:31.136
Now every row is calculated by our own function.

22
00:03:31.136 --> 00:03:33.360
Write it once, use it everywhere!

